Serbian law contains no offence of hitchhiking (''autostop''), and on ordinary roads it is legal. [https://www.pravno-informacioni-sistem.rs/SlGlasnikPortal/eli/rep/sgrs/skupstina/zakon/2009/41/3 Article 98 of the ''Zakon o bezbednosti saobraćaja na putevima''] is blunt about where it is not: a pedestrian "must not step onto or move along an ''autoput'' or a ''motoput''" — the motorway and the expressway — excepting only people such as accident investigators and road workers. That covers the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the slip roads of both, so on those roads there is nowhere a hitchhiker may lawfully stand. What is left is the signposted rest areas and petrol stations, where drivers have stopped anyway and you can ask them face to face. On state roads of the second order and everything below them, thumbing from the verge breaks no rule.
